Chinese 的/之 and Japanese の get negative baselines, weaker inside longer
tokens. Selected Japanese verb endings, noun-forming endings and particles
also get negative baselines. A recent 的/の adds a positive correction,
decaying with separation. Text following an earlier marker inside its token
counts as extra separation. The eight-token history resets per document.
